<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Hastings</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1066">1066</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Hastings was fought on 14 October 1066 near Hastings in Sussex, where Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William of Normandy</a> defeated and killed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The victory began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>: William was crowned king on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day 1066, and a new Norman aristocracy replaced the English nobility.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings.jpg" alt="Battle of Hastings" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Myrabella · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayeux_Tapestry_scene57_Harold_death.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Breton knights served widely abroad: a large Breton contingent fought for William of Normandy at the <b>Battle of Hastings</b> in 1066, and its leaders, such as Alan the Red, received great estates in England, including the honour of Richmond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Claiming the English throne, William assembled a fleet at Dives-sur-Mer, crossed from Saint-Valery-sur-Somme in late September 1066, and on 14 October defeated and killed Harold Godwinson at the <b>Battle of Hastings</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke William of Normandy defeated and killed Harold Godwinson at the <b>Battle of Hastings</b> on 14 October 1066, and on Christmas Day he was crowned king of England in Westminster Abbey, the church rebuilt by Edward the Confessor and consecrated only the year before.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>…Swedes joined the Byzantine emperor’s Varangian Guard, formed in the late 10th century, that a law of Västergötland barred anyone staying in “Greece” from inheriting, and Cnut’s conquest brought a royal guard of housecarls to England, which fought for Harold Godwinson at the <b>Battle of Hastings</b> in 1066.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li><li><blockquote>Just over two weeks later William won the <b>Battle of Hastings</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-28">28 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Eustace II fought for William the Conqueror at <b>Hastings</b> in 1066, and his sons Godfrey of Bouillon and Baldwin I led the First Crusade and ruled Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-boulogne">County of Boulogne</a></li></ul></section>
